Which of the following function types best models the relationship between hours and population?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Linear, because the population increases at each measurement"},{"key":"B","text":"Quadratic, because the first differences of the population values are increasing"},{"key":"C","text":"Exponential, because the population increases by approximately the same factor every $3$ hours"},{"key":"D","text":"Logarithmic, because the rate of population growth slows over time"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'Checking the ratios of consecutive population values: $\\frac{14}{5} = 2.8$, $\\frac{40}{14} \\approx 2.86$, $\\frac{113}{40} = 2.825$, and $\\frac{320}{113} \\approx 2.83$. Since the ratio is approximately constant ($\\approx 2.83$) over equal $3$-hour intervals, the data follows an exponential model. The option claiming a linear model because the population increases at each measurement confuses a necessary condition for linearity (consistent increase) with the actual requirement of constant first differences; the differences here are $9$, $26$, $73$, and $207$, which are far from constant. The option suggesting a quadratic model because first differences increase is not sufficient — a quadratic requires constant second differences, but the second differences ($17$, $47$, $134$) are not constant. The option proposing a logarithmic model contradicts the data, which shows accelerating growth rather than decelerating growth.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



